How to Set Up the Automated Pipeline

The Trigger (The Start Button)
I would set up a "Push Trigger." This means the second someone clicks "Commit" or "Merge" to the main branch, the pipeline automatically starts. No manual work is needed.

Environment Setup (The Workstation)
Since the code is running on a cloud server and not my laptop, I have to "build" the environment from scratch:
Tell the server to use Linux.
Install the software (like Node.js).
Download the libraries needed to run the tests.

Test Execution (The Work)
The server runs a single command (like npm test).
If the test passes, the pipeline keeps moving.
If the test fails, the pipeline stops immediately and alerts the team so broken code doesn't reach the customers.

Reporting (The Feedback)
Once finished, the pipeline saves a Test Report.
I would set it to save an HTML report as an "artifact" (a file you can download).
It sends a Slack or Email notification saying "Success" or "Failed."
